DATE='2026-11-06'
POSTS=[
('tax-planning-before-april-action-calendar','Tax Planning Before April: A Practical Action Calendar','Build a tax planning calendar that connects income projections, documentation, entity decisions and implementation before filing season.',
'https://medium.com/@AE_Tax_Advisors/tax-planning-should-happen-before-april-not-during-it-8d42f18d0d60','Tax Planning Should Happen Before April, Not During It','',[
('Start with a forecast you can actually use','Tax preparation reports what happened. Tax planning helps determine what to do while decisions are still available. A useful forecast combines year-to-date income, expected remaining income, payroll, investment activity and deductions supported by records. It should show both estimated liability and expected payments, because a lower balance due can result from higher withholding without any reduction in total tax.','For a business owner, begin with reconciled books and separate company profit from owner distributions. For an employee, gather recent pay statements, bonus expectations, equity compensation details and household income. A plan based on last year alone can miss a business acquisition, a property sale or a significant change in compensation.'),
('Use the middle of the year to make structural decisions','Entity classification, owner compensation and retirement plan design deserve time for analysis and implementation. Review the full cost of a proposed change, including payroll, state taxes, administration and future exit consequences. An LLC or corporate election does not create a deduction simply because paperwork was filed.','Ask for a written decision record: the proposed action, eligibility requirements, expected tax effect, cash required, responsible person and deadline. Some elections and contributions have deadlines after year-end, while other actions must occur during the tax year. The calendar should identify the actual rule for each item.'),
('Make year-end a completion checkpoint','Before December ends, confirm that planned actions occurred and collect the evidence. An equipment purchase may require the asset to be ready and available for business use before depreciation begins. A retirement strategy may require coordination with a plan administrator and payroll provider. Business reimbursements require a valid policy and substantiation.','Avoid spending a dollar solely to obtain a deduction worth a fraction of that dollar. Compare the after-tax cost with the business benefit and cash reserves. Tax savings should support a sound financial decision.'),
('Use filing season to reconcile the plan','When information returns arrive, compare them with the forecast. Reconcile estimated payments, withholding, carryforwards and entity returns before finalizing the individual return. Document differences so the next forecast starts from accurate information.','Bring your prior returns, current financials, pay statements and a list of upcoming transactions to an <a href="/discovery/">AE Tax Advisors discovery call</a>. The goal is a practical implementation sequence with evidence and deadlines, rather than a list of strategies that never reaches the return.')]),
('cost-segregation-study-worth-it-decision-checklist','Is Cost Segregation Worth It? A Property Owner’s Decision Checklist','Evaluate cost segregation using usable deductions, study fees, holding period, documentation and potential sale consequences.',
'https://medium.com/@AE_Tax_Advisors/230879488b39','Is a Cost Segregation Study Worth It? 2026 Guide','November 6, 2026 at 8:00 AM Eastern',[
('Measure the deduction you can use','Cost segregation identifies property components that may qualify for shorter depreciation recovery periods than the building. Its economic value depends on the timing of deductions and whether the taxpayer can use them. A large depreciation number is not the same thing as a large current refund.','Start with depreciable basis, excluding land, then review acquisition and placed-in-service dates, improvements, business use and prior depreciation. Applicable bonus depreciation rules depend on the property and relevant dates. A study should explain the classifications and supporting methodology rather than promise a percentage for every building.'),
('Run the limits before calculating savings','Basis, at-risk, passive activity and other loss limitations can delay a deduction. Rental losses generally do not automatically offset wages. Short-term rental treatment requires a separate factual analysis of the activity and participation; buying a furnished property does not resolve those requirements.','Have the return preparer model the study alongside the household return. Ask how much additional depreciation is usable this year, how much carries forward, and whether state depreciation rules differ from federal rules.'),
('Compare timing benefits with fees and exit taxes','Suppose an additional $80,000 deduction is currently usable at an assumed 30% marginal rate. The initial federal tax reduction would be approximately $24,000 before state effects and other interactions. This is an illustration, not a study estimate. If the loss is suspended, the immediate benefit may be much smaller or zero.','Compare that timing benefit with the study fee, implementation cost and planned holding period. A future sale may produce depreciation recapture or other taxable gain. Accelerating deductions often changes when tax is paid, so a lifetime model is more informative than the first-year deduction alone.'),
('Prepare the property file before commissioning a study','Collect closing statements, an allocation of land and improvements, renovation invoices, photographs, leases, prior depreciation schedules and business-use records. For property already depreciated, the preparer should determine whether an accounting method change, Form 3115 or another correction procedure applies.','Use the <a href="/cost-segregation-resources/">AE cost segregation resource</a> to start the discussion, then book a <a href="/discovery/">property-specific review</a>. A useful recommendation explains the tax treatment, current usability, documentation and sale assumptions together.')]),
('s-corp-vs-llc-tax-election-costs','S Corp vs LLC: Compare the Election, Payroll and Total Cost','Understand LLC legal status, S corporation taxation, reasonable compensation and the costs that determine whether an election makes sense.',
'https://medium.com/@AE_Tax_Advisors/e9e67ada4eab','S Corp vs LLC: Which Saves More Tax in 2026?','November 7, 2026 at 8:00 AM Eastern',[
('Separate legal structure from federal tax classification','An LLC is a legal entity formed under state law. S corporation status is a federal tax election available to qualifying entities. An eligible LLC can elect S corporation taxation while remaining an LLC under state law. The useful comparison is therefore the existing tax classification against an eligible S election.','A single-owner domestic LLC is generally disregarded for federal income tax unless it elects otherwise. A domestic LLC with multiple owners generally defaults to partnership treatment. Ownership, intended investments and state requirements can affect which alternatives are appropriate.'),
('Model reasonable wages before estimating payroll savings','An owner who performs services for an S corporation must receive reasonable compensation for those services before non-wage distributions. The amount depends on duties, time, experience, comparable pay and how the business generates revenue. There is no universal salary-to-distribution percentage that makes every return compliant.','Potential employment tax savings must be calculated after reasonable wages, employer payroll taxes, other wages and applicable limits. Distributions are not simply tax-free income: pass-through business income generally remains subject to income tax, and distribution treatment depends in part on shareholder basis.'),
('Include the recurring cost of the election','An S corporation typically adds payroll filings, a separate federal return, compensation documentation and more bookkeeping. State taxes and entity-level fees can materially change the result. Retirement contributions, health insurance reporting and the qualified business income deduction also require coordination.','Consider an illustrative business expecting $160,000 before owner compensation. If reasonable wages and related costs absorb most of that amount, the remaining benefit may not justify added administration. A larger profit does not settle the question either: the owner’s role, state and income mix still matter.'),
('Choose based on the operating business and its future','Review eligibility, election timing, ownership restrictions, financing plans and a potential sale before making a change. Businesses holding appreciating real estate require particular care because later transfers and distributions can have consequences that differ across structures.','Bring current profit, ownership details, owner duties and existing returns to an <a href="/discovery/">AE entity review</a>. Request a side-by-side projection showing total federal and state tax, administration and cash left to the owner under each realistic alternative.')]),
('amended-tax-return-deadline-records-checklist','Amended Tax Return Deadlines: Build a Year-by-Year Review File','Review amendment eligibility, refund deadlines, payment lookback limits, supporting documents and state return effects.',
'https://medium.com/@AE_Tax_Advisors/54551ee7a86a','How Far Back Can You Amend a Tax Return?','November 8, 2026 at 8:00 AM Eastern',[
('Identify the change before choosing a filing year','An amendment review begins with a specific error or omission: missing income, an incorrect filing status, an eligible deduction, a credit or a corrected information return. Compare the filed return with source records and quantify the proposed change. Do not assume every earlier year is available for a refund.','For an individual federal refund claim, the general filing deadline is the later of three years after filing the original return or two years after paying the tax. Early-filed returns are generally treated as filed on the due date. Exceptions and payment lookback rules can change the result.'),
('Separate the filing deadline from the refundable amount','A timely claim does not by itself establish the amount recoverable. The refund is also subject to rules limiting which payments fall within the applicable lookback period. Extensions, payment dates and special circumstances need to be reviewed using the actual record.','Create one row for each tax year with the original filing date, extension, tax payment dates, proposed adjustment, supporting documents and calculated refund deadline. Obtain account transcripts when necessary to verify filing and payment history.'),
('Reconcile entity and individual returns together','A business return correction may require revised owner reporting and related state amendments. Review carryforwards across subsequent years so the same deduction or credit is not claimed twice. Partnership corrections may require an administrative adjustment request rather than a conventional amended return, depending on the applicable regime.','Depreciation errors also require procedural analysis. An accounting method change can be relevant in circumstances where simply amending a prior return is not the correct solution. The procedure should follow the facts, prior treatment and applicable rules.'),
('Prepare an amendment package that a reviewer can trace','Gather the filed federal and state returns, all schedules, original and corrected information returns, payment evidence, relevant invoices and a clear explanation of each change. Keep a reconciliation showing original amounts, corrected amounts and the difference.','Ask AE to evaluate both eligibility and economics through a <a href="/discovery/">return review call</a>. The outcome should identify supported changes, missing evidence, filing procedures, deadlines and expected federal and state effects. A refund estimate remains provisional until the complete return is modeled.')]),
('small-business-tax-moves-implementation-guide','Small Business Tax Moves: Turn a Checklist Into an Implemented Plan','Connect small business bookkeeping, expenses, reimbursements, payroll and retirement decisions with an accountable implementation process.',
'https://sites.google.com/aetaxadvisors.com/smallbusinesstaxmoves','Small Business Tax Moves','',[
('Start with reliable books and a clear baseline','Small business tax planning works best when the financial statements reflect actual activity. Reconcile bank and credit card accounts, separate owner transfers from operating expenses, and review loan balances. A loan principal payment generally is not a deductible operating expense simply because cash left the account.','Build a projection before choosing a strategy. Estimate annual profit, owner compensation, household income and taxes already paid. This baseline shows whether a proposed action changes taxable income, payment timing or only the amount due at filing.'),
('Document expenses and reimbursements as they happen','Review ordinary and necessary business expenses with receipts and a recorded business purpose. Vehicle use, travel and mixed personal-business costs need appropriate allocation. A company bank account does not convert a personal expense into a deductible business expense.','A properly administered accountable plan can reimburse qualifying employee business expenses when its requirements are met. This includes substantiation and the return of excess advances. Owners should review their entity type and employee status before applying a reimbursement policy.'),
('Coordinate owner pay, retirement and equipment decisions','Payroll and retirement choices interact. Changing wages can affect employment taxes and the compensation available for plan contributions. Plan design also must account for eligible employees, deadlines, administration and available cash. Coordinate the return preparer, payroll provider and plan administrator before implementation.','For equipment, confirm the business need and placed-in-service date. Compare depreciation choices with expected income and future sale plans. A deduction should improve the economics of an asset the business needs; it should not be the only reason for purchasing it.'),
('Assign a deadline and evidence to every action','Turn the plan into a short implementation register: action, owner, expected benefit, cash requirement, due date and proof of completion. Revisit the register when profit, staffing or ownership changes. At filing, compare the final return with the projection and explain any differences.','The companion <a href="https://sites.google.com/aetaxadvisors.com/smallbusinesstaxmoves">Small Business Tax Moves resource on Google Sites</a> provides another entry point into AE’s educational materials. Use this article to move from a topic list to a documented process, then <a href="/discovery/">discuss your business with AE</a>.')]),
('reduce-w2-taxes-household-planning-guide','Reduce W-2 Taxes: A Household Planning Guide for High Earners','Review workplace benefits, household income, investment decisions and rental loss limits before choosing a W-2 tax strategy.',
'https://sites.google.com/aetaxadvisors.com/reduceyourw-2taxes','Reduce Your W-2 Taxes','',[
('Begin with the income you actually have','W-2 planning starts with employee compensation, workplace benefits and the household’s other income. Salary, bonuses, equity compensation, a spouse’s wages and investment gains can create very different planning needs. Gather pay statements and benefit elections before estimating savings.','Changing Form W-4 withholding adjusts payments toward tax. It does not by itself reduce the underlying liability. Compare total projected tax with withholding and estimated payments so cash-flow changes are not mistaken for permanent savings.'),
('Review workplace benefits before adding complexity','Eligible pretax retirement contributions can reduce current federal taxable wages, subject to the applicable plan and tax-year rules. Roth contributions have a different tax treatment. Health savings account contributions require eligibility, including qualifying coverage and the absence of disqualifying coverage.','Review employer plans, contribution records and household coverage before assuming a deduction is available. Contributions, employer funding and eligibility can interact. A strategy should fit liquidity needs and long-term savings goals as well as the current tax return.'),
('Evaluate side businesses and real estate on their facts','Forming an LLC does not turn employee wages into business income or create deductions for personal spending. A real side business must be evaluated using its actual income, expenses and activities. Entity planning should follow those facts.','Rental depreciation does not automatically offset wages. Passive activity rules often restrict rental losses, and exceptions require careful analysis. Short-term rentals require review of average stays, services, participation and other limitations. A property purchase should also make financial sense before any tax benefit is considered.'),
('Use one household projection to compare alternatives','Investment gains, charitable giving, retirement elections and business activity should be modeled together. A deduction may reduce taxable income without reducing adjusted gross income, and some benefits are limited by income or other requirements. State treatment can differ from federal treatment.','Review the companion <a href="https://sites.google.com/aetaxadvisors.com/reduceyourw-2taxes">Reduce Your W-2 Taxes resource on Google Sites</a>, then bring your pay statements, prior return and upcoming transactions to an <a href="/discovery/">AE discovery call</a>. Request a projection showing eligibility, cash required, implementation steps and estimated total tax under each option.')])]
